<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Simbel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Simbel is a site on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, with two rock-cut temples built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> in the 13th century BCE; the larger is fronted by four colossal seated statues of the king. To save them from the rising waters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a>, both temples were cut into blocks and rebuilt on higher ground in 1964–1968 in an international campaign led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-simbel.jpg" alt="Abu Simbel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: youssef_alam · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramsis,_Aswan_Governorate,_Egypt_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
